Article 11. Where a capital contributor makes a capital contribution with equity of another company, and the following conditions are met, the People's Court shall determine that the capital contributor has performed its capital contribution obligation:
(1) The equity contributed is lawfully held by the capital contributor and may be transferred according to law;
(2) The equity contributed has no rights defects or rights burdens;
(3) The capital contributor has completed the legal formalities for the transfer of equity;
(4) The equity contributed has been valued in accordance with law.
Where equity contribution does not conform to the provisions of items (1), (2), and (3) of the preceding paragraph, and the company, other shareholders, or creditors of the company request that it be determined that the capital contributor has failed to perform its capital contribution obligation, the People's Court shall order the capital contributor to take remedial measures within a specified reasonable period to meet the above conditions; if no remedy is made within the time limit, the People's Court shall determine that it has not fully performed its capital contribution obligation according to law.
Where equity contribution does not conform to the provisions of item (4) of the first paragraph of this Article, and the company, other shareholders, or creditors of the company request that it be determined that the capital contributor has failed to perform its capital contribution obligation, the People's Court shall handle the matter in accordance with the provisions of Article 9 of these Provisions.

Article 12. After a company is established, where the company, shareholders, or creditors of the company, on the ground that the conduct of a relevant shareholder falls under any of the following circumstances and harms the company's rights and interests, request that it be determined that the shareholder withdrew its capital contribution, the People's Court shall support such request:
(1) Making false financial and accounting statements to inflate profits for distribution;
(2) Transferring out its capital contribution by fabricating a creditor-debt relationship;
(3) Transferring out its capital contribution through affiliated transactions;
(4) Other acts of withdrawing capital contribution without going through statutory procedures.

Article 22. Where the parties dispute the ownership of equity, and one party requests the People's Court to confirm that it enjoys the equity, it shall prove one of the following facts:
(1) It has already made a capital contribution or subscribed for a capital contribution to the company according to law, and does not violate the mandatory provisions of laws and regulations;
(2) It has already acquired the company's equity by transfer or other forms of succession, and does not violate the mandatory provisions of laws and regulations.
